      ":question/id": "1abca24a-bd18-5df4-80b7-52b463869515",
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y=2x-3\\operatorname{arcsec}x$ at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The inverse secant derivative is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsec}x)=\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{x^2-1}}.\n$$\n\nApply the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny'(x)&=2-3\\left(\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{x^2-1}}\\right)\\\\\n&=2-\\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{x^2-1}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the specified input, $|-\\sqrt{3}|=\\sqrt{3}$ and $(-\\sqrt{3})^2-1=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=y'(-\\sqrt{3})&=2-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{2}}\\\\\n&=2-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}\\\\\n&=2-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value keeps the denominator positive even though the input is negative. Since $-\\sqrt{3}<-1$, the derivative exists here."
    },
